<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Monthly Rental</span>
<h1>Expats: Choosing a Monthly Rental Bike in Hanoi | Hanoi Motorbike Guide</h1>
<p>An expat renting by the month is not borrowing a machine for a holiday, they are adopting their daily transport, and the choice deserves the seriousness any daily tool deserves. The right bike for a long stay is the one that matches the riding you actually do, the commute, the errands, the weekend loops, rather than the machine that flattered you in the shop window, and the wrong one announces itself politely in the second week and rudely by the second month. This page walks the choosing honestly, model by model, with the verified monthly prices attached, and with the questions the desk at a real rental counter answers better than any page can.</p>
<p>The machines in this guide come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu at 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, a single counter, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the month&rsquo;s terms are confirmed in person. Start with the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental in Hanoi</a> hub for the long-stay picture, compare the numbers in the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> guide, see the fleet and terms at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> page, read the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/motorbike-rental-for-expats-hanoi/">motorbike rental for expats</a> guide for the settling-in context, and browse the wider <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> as the questions narrow.</p>
<nav class="toc">
<ol>
<li><a href="#sec-1">What a month of riding actually asks</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-2">The budget tier and its honest case</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-3">The premium scooters for long commutes</a></li>
